  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Basically, it would be nice to have a better avatar tag filter system. NSFW avatars for example, could have this tag. If a user wishes to not see this content, they can blacklist this tag. \nThis will allow everyone to be happy. Other forms of tags could go into this as well, for example:\nGore, Cruel Content, Flashy Lights, Screen Effects, Loud Noise, Particle Systems, Long Range Audio, etc.\nThis would clearly be great for people who suffer from medical conditions, or mental struggles if they wish to block some content. This would also allow \"Wholesome\" content to be used safely and respectfully in that kind of group. Basically making everyone happy. \nThis would also allow content creators like me, to recommend my avatars to the correct group of people with some form of avatar search, it would also allow me to block my content for users who suffer conditions in order to keep them safe. It would also be nice if we could connect an age system with our accounts? So some forms of content/tags are blocked by default and are only used by the correct age group. Of course, people will lie about their age sometimes. But that's just the internet. As of today, underage users still play VRChat and though moderated it's something hard to keep track of, so it would be nice to put some form of filter in that community controlled space. If the community can do something better, let us have that control. \nNSFW Content Specific:\nAs for NSFW flagged avatars, when with the content enabled there should be a warning system and they should be blocked by default. You would need to enable the avatar, and when doing so a window should popup saying, \"This Content is flagged as \"NSFW\", meaning it may contain age restricted content. Show at your own script\" with a \"Show Avatar\" or \"Cancel\" confirmation.\nThis also puts more control into staff hands, as if admins come across unflagged nsfw content, the user can have a warning for flagging content incorrectly and Admin's can manually add the NSFW tag.\nTo sum this up, it basically helps everyone really and is just something that we should have always had implemented correctly. (Totally not laughing at the old SDK upload menu with NSFW tags that do nothing.)",
